{%- comment -%}
  ═══ VAULT FORK DISABLED 2026-08-23 — ONE PDP FOR EVERY PRODUCT ═══

  This line used to read:
      {% if product.tags contains 'vault' or product.price >= 70000 %}
  which sent anything tagged `vault` OR priced at/above 700 MAD to a completely
  separate, bespoke PDP in snippets/lbass-pdp-vault.liquid. Two PDPs, no shared
  code, so every product-page improvement had to be built twice — and in
  practice was not: the vault branch shipped for months with no Product schema,
  no BreadcrumbList and no image lightbox at all.

  Owner asked for the vault pages to look like every other product page.
  Setting the flag below to false routes them through the standard branch.

  NOTHING IS LOST BY DOING THIS — checked before switching:
    • schema      the standard branch emits Product + Offer + BreadcrumbList,
                  with MORE detail than the vault version ever did (measurements,
                  fit notes, flaws, ticket status, gated aggregateRating)
    • lightbox    the original, well-tested one (.zoomlb)
    • condition   reads lbass.condition_status natively, with the new/used
                  conditional wording — the vault copy of this logic was the
                  one that had been reading the wrong metafield namespace
    • tracking    ZERO vault events live in that snippet. lbass_vault_view and
                  lbass_vault_main_cta_click fire from templates/index.liquid,
                  lbass_vault_product_click from page.vault + collection,
                  lbass_vault_checkout_start from cart + cart-drawer. All
                  untouched.

  What DOES go away is the vault visual identity — the gold "VAULT · VENDOR"
  label, the Passport block and the Home › VAULT › vendor breadcrumb. That is
  the point of the change, not a side effect.

  /pages/vault (templates/page.vault.liquid) is a separate page and still works.

  TO REVERT: set use_vault_pdp back to true. The snippet is untouched on disk.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign use_vault_pdp = false -%}
{% if use_vault_pdp %}
  {% render 'lbass-pdp-vault' %}
{% else %}
{% comment %}
  BREADCRUMB COLLECTION — must not be an internal/admin collection.

  This was `product.collections.first`, which is arbitrary. Measured 2026-08-05:
  it resolved to "Audited Drop P001-P012" on the Zara jacket and "Home page" on
  the Adidas track pants — both internal buckets, not categories a shopper or
  Google would recognise. That string feeds the visible breadcrumb AND
  BreadcrumbList schema on every one of the 40 product pages.

  Now: skip the junk handles, prefer a real category, fall back to a brand
  collection, and only then to whatever exists. `all` is excluded too — a
  breadcrumb saying "كل القطع" tells nobody anything.

  The published `nike` and `adidas-originals` collections are valid breadcrumb
  candidates. A prior override pointed them at nonexistent /brand-* handles;
  that was removed after browser verification on 2026-08-20.
{% endcomment %}
{%- assign bc_junk = 'frontpage,all,audited-drop-p001-p012' | split: ',' -%}
{%- assign first_collection = nil -%}
{%- assign fallback_collection = nil -%}
{%- for c in product.collections -%}
  {%- unless bc_junk contains c.handle -%}
    {%- if fallback_collection == nil -%}{%- assign fallback_collection = c -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- comment -%} prefer a category over a brand bucket {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- unless c.handle contains 'brand-' -%}
      {%- if first_collection == nil -%}{%- assign first_collection = c -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- if first_collection == nil -%}{%- assign first_collection = fallback_collection -%}{%- endif -%}
